---
// `/map` moved to `/`, and its query string has to move with it.
//
// The map was an instrument at this path for the life of the site, and it is now the index.
// Nothing about it was withdrawn — it changed position — so a link somebody kept should still
// arrive where it pointed.
//
// **Which is why this is not an `astro.config` redirect.** Those emit a fixed
// `<meta http-equiv="refresh" content="0;url=/">`, and a fixed URL drops everything after the
// `?`. The map keeps its whole view in the query string, so a reader who kept
// `/map?year=1885&at=site/lima-refinery.yml` would land on the present with no year, no
// selection, and nothing anywhere saying a link had been lost.
//
// Two mechanisms, in the order they fire:
//
//   1. A script in `<head>` replaces the location with the query and hash intact. It runs before
//      the body paints, so a reader with JavaScript never sees this page.
//   2. The meta refresh behind it, which is the no-JS path. It cannot carry the query — that is
//      the whole limitation above — so the fallback is the map's default view, and the link on
//      the page says where it is going.
//
// `check-outline.mjs` exempts a redirect stub from the one-`h1` rule by looking for that meta
// tag, and a page that stops being a redirect stops being exempt.
---

<meta http-equiv="refresh" content="0;url=/" />
<meta name="robots" content="noindex" />
<title>Redirecting to the map</title>
<script is:inline>
  // `replace`, not `assign`: this page should not sit in the reader's history for the back
  // button to land on and bounce off again.
  location.replace('/' + location.search + location.hash)
</script>
<body>
  <p>
    The map is now at <a href="/">the front of the site</a>.
  </p>
</body>
